<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/750">750</a>–<a href="/years/754">754</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as al-Saffah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, reigning from 750 to 754. He was proclaimed caliph during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-revolution">Abbasid Revolution</a>, and his forces defeated the last Umayyad caliph. His short reign saw the consolidation of Abbasid power and the elimination of many members of the Umayyad family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-al-abbas-al-saffah.jpg" alt="Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abu Ali Bal'ami · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balami_-_Tarikhnama_-_Abu%27l-%27Abbas_al-Saffah_is_proclaimed_the_first_%27Abbasid_Caliph_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Saffah was born at al-Humayma, in present-day Jordan, in 721 or 722. He headed one branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a>, a sub-clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> descended from Hashim, a great-grandfather of Muhammad, through al-Abbas, an uncle of the Prophet; hence the name Abbasid. This indirect link to Muhammad's clan was the basis of his claim to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>.</p><p>Many believed, on the strength of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, that in the end times a leader or mahdi from Muhammad's family would deliver <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from corrupt leadership. The Umayyad caliphs' half-hearted tolerance of non-Arab Muslims and Shi'as had failed to quell unrest, which produced a Shi'a revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> under Zayd ibn Ali, put down in 740. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> chose to begin their rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, a remote but important military region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In early October 749 al-Saffah's army entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>. In February 750 it defeated the Umayyad caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marwan-ii">Marwan II</a> at the Battle of the Zab, effectively ending a dynasty that had ruled since 661. Marwan fled and was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> that August. Al-Saffah did not come forward to receive the people's pledge of allegiance until Marwan and many Umayyad princes were dead. He made Kufa his capital, ending the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and called himself al-Saffah, the Blood-Shedder, in his inaugural address in its Great Mosque.</p><p>Later tales say he invited the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> to a dinner and had them clubbed to death; the one survivor, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiya, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, where Umayyad rule lasted three more centuries. Apart from his policy toward the Umayyads, historians widely regard him as a mild victor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Eastern Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> served in his government, and his army admitted non-Muslims and non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, whom the Umayyads had refused. In 751 Abbasid forces defeated the Tang army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a>, after the Karluk Turks changed sides; Muslim control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Saffah died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> on 8 June 754, on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. His own son was too young, so he named his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ja">Abu Ja</a>'far as heir. His uncle Abd Allah ibn Ali, who had assembled an army for a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, claiming al-Saffah had promised him the succession. Abu Ja'far, who took the name al-Mansur, ruled until 775; he imprisoned his uncle in 754 and had him killed in 764.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Saffah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first caliph, <b>Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah</b> (r. 750–754), inherited the Umayyad territorial empire and faced challenges at once.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Humayma, in what is now <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a>. Died in Al Anbar Governorate, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.95,35.35&amp;year=752" aria-label="Humayma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abu al-Abbas al-Saffah"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5396 4995h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5396 4995h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.95,35.35&amp;year=752">What was here in 752 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
